<script>
  import streamSlow from "$server/rpc/streamSlow"
  import streamBoom from "$server/rpc/streamBoom"
  import streamBranch from "$server/rpc/streamBranch"
  import { state } from "abide/shared/state"

  // TRUE at SSR on purpose: the third block below has to actually render on the server for the
  // branch-arm case to be exercised at all.
  let branchOpen = state(true)
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Streaming SSR</h1>
  <p class="lead">A page renders on the server first. A <code>{'{'}#await{'}'}</code> read that settles within the render deadline lands inline in the initial HTML; one that misses it doesn't block the response — abide flushes the shell with a placeholder, then <strong>streams</strong> the resolved branch in as an out-of-order patch. Hydration claims the streamed nodes in place, so the block stays fully reactive.</p>

  <h2>Slow read — shell, then streamed patch</h2>
  <p>This <code>{'{'}#await{'}'}</code> read misses the render deadline, so SSR flushes the shell with a placeholder and streams the resolved branch in. Hydration claims the streamed nodes in place, so the block stays reactive — refresh re-fetches and re-renders it.</p>

  <div data-testid="slow-block" class="rounded border border-slate-200 p-4">
    {#await streamSlow()}
      <span data-testid="pending">loading…</span>
    {:then result}
      <b data-testid="value">runs: {result.runs}</b>
    {:catch failure}
      <span data-testid="error">{failure.message}</span>
    {/await}
  </div>

  <button
    data-testid="refresh"
    class="mt-4 rounded bg-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 text-white"
    onclick={() => streamSlow.refresh()}
  >
    Refresh
  </button>

  <h2 class="mt-8">Rejected read — the streamed <code>{'{'}:catch{'}'}</code></h2>
  <p>A streamed read that <em>rejects</em> after the shell flushed streams its <code>{'{'}:catch{'}'}</code> branch as the patch — no 500 (headers are already out), and the client hydrates the catch branch in place.</p>

  <div data-testid="error-block" class="rounded border border-slate-200 p-4">
    {#await streamBoom()}
      <span>loading…</span>
    {:then ok}
      <b>ok: {ok.ok}</b>
    {:catch failure}
      <i data-testid="error-value">{failure.message}</i>
    {/await}
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-8">Streamed from inside an <code>{'{'}#if{'}'}</code> branch</h2>
  <p>A streaming read does not have to sit directly under an element. Inside an <code>{'{'}#if{'}'}</code> — or a <code>{'{'}:case{'}'}</code>, or a <code>{'{'}:catch{'}'}</code> — it streams exactly the same way: the branch keeps its own render frame, so the shell still flushes with a placeholder and the resolved branch still arrives as an out-of-order patch.</p>

  <div data-testid="branch-block" class="rounded border border-slate-200 p-4">
    {#if branchOpen}
      {#await streamBranch()}
        <span data-testid="branch-pending">loading…</span>
      {:then result}
        <b data-testid="branch-value">runs: {result.runs}</b>
      {:catch failure}
        <span data-testid="branch-error">{failure.message}</span>
      {/await}
    {:else}
      <span data-testid="branch-closed">closed</span>
    {/if}
  </div>
</main>
